# FRAME .gitattributes -- every text file in the repo is checked out with LF, on every platform,
# whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# Why (measured 2026-10-12): git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true and turns LF into CRLF on checkout;
#   every ops/verify/*.sh then dies in bash with `set: -: invalid option` / `syntax error near $'do\r'`,
#   so **not one gate in the whole table runs** (Git Bash, WSL, a cloud session mounting the folder -- all the same);
#   a Linux git looking at the mounted folder also reports the whole tree as "modified" (only the line endings differ).
# Editors, PowerShell and the Rust/Go/Node toolchains on Windows all accept LF, so LF everywhere costs nothing.
#
# Add your project's own rules below (this file is seed: sync creates it when missing and never overwrites it).
# After adding it, a working tree already checked out as CRLF needs one refresh:
#   git rm -r --cached -q . && git reset --hard      (commit or stash your own changes first)

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files must be CRLF (cmd.exe misreads labels/jumps in an LF .bat).
# They are committed as CRLF already, so -text: in as-is, out as-is, git converts nothing.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text
